# PR67 CI107 independent triage

Reviewer: `codex-pr67-independent-final-auditor`, kind `agent`. Triage began2026-11-08T11:36:46Z. This is a pre-stamp causal/implementation review, not a renewed Final Audit or a passed release. I read source, the actual CI log, local observer data, local trace JSON/network records, the controlled red trace/error context and current proposed changes. I did not run a server, alter source/tests/settings, download inaccessible CI artifacts or mutate the earlier final-audit report/result. Only this outside-repository triage report is written.

**Disposition:** the proposed narrow correction addresses two independently identifiable Laboratory navigation weaknesses without weakening the inherited substantive assertions. The stale-media product callback is a demonstrated vulnerability; the toggle-as-close helper is unsafe under a demonstrated breakpoint-state interval. The exact original CI event interleaving and the original extra-tab missing-link cause remain **OPEN/unverified**. Green local reruns do not establish those causes or replace exact-head full CI. A new stamp, separate correction audit and complete approved CI/strict remain required.

## Actual failed CI evidence

The supplied run/job identities are42473433589/128709343906, associated PR head `aa61600822ccdd998ea001b834b58b16dc332225`. The actual CI checkout is synthetic merge `747912f08e8ffcccc4a42af5bd083f7abfacc6e3`, merging that head into base `9252739eb1bdf7bf6aabc2696ae57000aa02a49f` (raw log lines313–316). Do not label a future corrected head as this tested revision.

The actual CI log concludes **431 passed, two flaky, one failed,24.1min**, child exit1, from the434-case approved profile. The compact Compute keyboard/Properties/Networking case permanently failed. Its retry logs resolve the Networking button as visible/enabled/stable, then repeatedly identify an `aside.portal-nav.mobile-open` subtree intercepting pointer events. The desktop execution of that same viewport-changing case was flaky. The Firewall overlapping-hub extra-tab case was flaky at `revealLaboratoryLink`: Virtual networks link not found/visible within5000ms after `editor.goto('/labs/laboratory')`. These are browser evidence failures, not evidence that Firewall route decisions, PostgreSQL replay or VM creation rules failed. Full check and earlier CI steps passed; the failed browser run cannot supply a successful candidate browser receipt, and strict release was not reached/passed.

The raw CI log inspected here is `/workspace/azurelab-pr67/ci107/ci-job.log.raw`, SHA256 `1a72ecdaa5e3c642ec031bb367613d8aa817fd1d22c74b25017609d5bf4334c4`. I have not inspected the original CI trace. The supplied artifact identity is13615640165, reported about587MB; the parent reports an MCP512MB limit and a platform-blocked Azure Blob CONNECT on the actual redirect. The local `playwright-report.zip` is zero bytes, not a valid retrieved archive. No bypass was attempted in this review; no local trace is relabelled an original CI trace.

## What the local evidence proves

The pre-correction instrumented copies passed five compact Compute and three desktop Firewall repetitions. These were useful observations, not reproduced CI failures. I independently parsed all five Compute `dom-records.json` files: each contains a real transition at width1024/media=true with `aria-expanded="true"`, `navClass="portal-nav "`, and nav hidden. Immediately afterward local records change expanded to false. This demonstrates that CSS/native media state can update before the Laboratory React `narrow` state drives the header. An example trace records the mismatch at6103.2ms and React-corrected header at6104.6ms. The observer may itself affect timing; these values do not reveal the inaccessible CI ordering.

The original header callback branches on captured React `narrow` and uses captured toggle values. The old close helper branches on actual `innerWidth`, reads header `aria-expanded`, and clicks the same toggle if true, without asserting that the drawer is closed. At the compact breakpoint, the header's old desktop-expanded value is not an unambiguous indication that the compact drawer is open. A helper can read desktop `true`, then click after React has switched to compact, turning `menu` on and leaving an overlay across Networking. This is a valid source-level mechanism consistent with the CI symptom; it is **not a reconstructed exact CI event sequence**.

The controlled permanent test holds delivery only of real `MediaQueryList` change callbacks for the exact1100px query. Native `matchMedia().matches`, viewport size and CSS continue to change. It does not inject a positive World, alter receipts, fake API outcomes or raise timeouts. The retained actual red trace shows: ready3.5 workspace; hold callback delivery; native resize to1024×500; nav hidden; menu toggle click; nav still hidden;5000ms visible assertion failure. Source inspection explains this: old `narrow=false` directs the click into desktop collapse, never opening the compact menu. That is a demonstrated product callback vulnerability, independently of whether it caused this CI overlay.

The proposed test now also explicitly remembers the old helper's stale desktop-true decision, releases real media delivery, waits for compact-false header state, then acts on the remembered decision by toggling. The real drawer opens, and Escape plus actual drawer postconditions must dismiss it. This separately exposes the old helper's read-before-delivery/action-after-delivery hazard. World, events and course local-storage bytes are compared before/after. This is a controlled vulnerability-class regression, not a claim that the original CI trace was read.

Local red log SHA256: `db5f9a9c5965eb9efd876c66f02ccc0c25119273e849f57e119a733388b8afb0`. Five-Compute probe log SHA256: `db11ff4eb7ff043b25b0e772ae9ca6401d31432cce69a8b59ab13ee40c52bef1`. Three-Firewall probe log SHA256: `d319b29e815428124210c187cc8253c45a70c524371c001595542e0624fa0603`.

## Extra-tab readiness: exact limit

The initial helper inspects nav visibility before explicitly waiting for the resumed workspace. A Portal frame and base navigation can exist while `loading=true`/`snapshot=null`; model-derived Virtual networks links are not yet available. In one actual local Firewall trace, editor `goto` starts17725.542ms, nav visibility is queried18115.951ms, and the editor frame's actual `/api/laboratory/state` request starts18191.926ms, returns200 in28.14ms. The link-visible assertion starts18298.192ms and then succeeds. This independently confirms the general readiness distinction; it does not reproduce the CI failure or prove that CI used the same timing, response or model.

**Important retention gap:** each inspected Firewall probe trace has only one `lab-dom` JSON attachment (258 records) for the surviving main page. The test closes `editor` before `afterEach` iterates `context.pages()`. Therefore the closed editor's `__labNotes` are not attached. Main-page mutation observations cannot substantiate an assertion about that editor's original missing link. Browser traces/network include local editor actions and state request evidence, but those are successful local runs, not original CI failure context. DOM notes also reset on document reload and are not automatically a complete cross-reload history.

The added helper fence—actual visible Laboratory frame and absence of the visible loading status before choosing navigation visibility—is a source-supported semantic prerequisite. It retains the actual link assertion and its usual bound. If the resumed workspace is unavailable, wrong-model, corrupt or a link is genuinely missing after loading, that assertion must still fail. It should not be described as closing the exact original extra-tab cause. The parent has explicitly agreed to retain that cause OPEN; no broader speculative investigation is needed unless subsequent checks reveal a serious defect. If future recurrence requires deeper triage, capture editor notes before `editor.close()` and retain its state response/DOM at failure.

## Proposed correction review

- **Product:** at toggle click, use current native1100px `matchMedia` result; synchronize `narrow`; use functional menu/collapsed setters. This aligns the operation with the CSS breakpoint at the actual interaction and avoids captured toggle values. The code remains in Laboratory, not shared Portal/course runtime. Escape sets `menu=false` idempotently and follows the already-existing course Portal dismissal behavior. It does not execute any technical command, change profile, reset evidence or alter CSS.
- **Helper:** wait for actual frame/loading readiness, retain the requested visible-link assertion, and at compact widths press Escape with both `mobile-open` absence and actual invisibility postconditions. This strengthens state verification instead of accepting toggle intent. It removes reliance on ambiguous desktop `aria-expanded` during transition. It neither forces a pointer click nor hides an overlay to make Networking pass.
- **Scope:** I inspected the diff against the submitted head: Laboratory callback/effect, shared Laboratory-only browser helper, one added controlled native case, and explicit preservation coverage for that helper. Existing Compute test body is unchanged. At this triage point domain/SQL/repository/course/shared presentation/CSS and QA configurations are untouched. The new profile is planned435 (391desktop/44compact), retaining426 inherited executions. Full plan/preservation evidence must be generated against the final candidate; this unstamped triage is not that receipt.
- **Test precision:** the wrapper retains native media matches and defers only callback delivery. Its listener wrapping is adequate for the single tested listener and is not a replacement production media implementation. The new second phase's old-helper decision is explicitly controlled; it should retain that description, rather than being called a naturally reproduced original CI failure.

The available `menu-corrected.log` now reports four affected native passes in59.9s: desktop Compute compact/short case, Firewall overlapping-hub case, controlled menu case and compact Compute. The parent subsequently expanded the controlled test's second phase; that final version still needs its affected run/full check and renewed independent stamped audit. I inspected current code and retained runs but did not start a competing server or claim these are my independently executed correction runs. Build/type helper-nullability errors are separate diagnostic/implementation failures, not original CI reproductions.

## Required honest disposition

1. Record the first authoritative CI run as failed, with original permanent failure/flaky results and skipped strict gate; retain actual tested merge/head identity.
2. Mark the stale-media callback and toggle-as-close vulnerabilities as concretely demonstrated; close their correction only after the final code, permanent controlled case and relevant native/preservation checks are independently verified on a renewed stamp.
3. Keep **original CI interleaving**, **original extra-tab missing-link cause**, and older VM duplicate-status/Compute/Axe uncertainties OPEN when unproven. Local green repetitions and a corrected CI pass do not retroactively establish causality.
4. Require renewed full check/stamp, independent follow-up and one complete approved exact-head CI/strict execution. Retain unchanged test limits/old assertions and do not use targeted passes as the full browser receipt.

No additional serious defect was identified in this proposed narrow correction during this read-only triage. This is not final acceptance of its unstamped bytes or permission to merge/deploy.

